[# Section 3 — surfaces and projections.
   Authored 2026-05-22. Anchored in spec/canonical.md + spec/conversions.md. #]

[section id=surfaces n=3 title='Surfaces and projections'

  [child id=cx-canonical n=3.1 title='CX — the canonical dialect'
    [intro
      """CX is itself the canonical surface. Every other supported format
         is defined as a projection of canonical CX. The normative rules
         live in [`spec/canonical.md`](../../spec/canonical.md); this
         section is the working summary."""]

    [body
      """Canonical CX has the following discipline:"""]

    [list
      [item """**One quote form per string.** Triple-quoted for multi-line
              bodies; single-quoted (`'…'`) for single-line strings. Double
              quotes are accepted on input but emitted as single quotes."""]
      [item """**Attributes sorted by name.** Within an element, attributes
              emit in lexicographic order. Source-order is not preserved
              at the canonical layer — only at the parser level."""]
      [item """**No insignificant whitespace.** Bodies are joined with
              single spaces; structural newlines exist only where the
              parser requires them. The canonical form is unique."""]
      [item """**ID anchors emit before other attributes.** `#name`
              precedes any `:attr value` pair on the same element."""]
      [item """**No comments.** Canonical bytes omit `[# … #]`. Comments
              are preserved by `cx fmt` (the non-canonical pretty-printer)
              but stripped by `cx canon`."""]]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# Source — author-friendly form #]
         [user
           :email 'ada@example.com'
           :id 42
           +active]

         [# Canonical form — `cx canon` output #]
         [user :active true :email 'ada@example.com' :id 42]"""]

    [body
      """The canonical bytes are what the SHA-256 hash is taken over (see
         [[identity]]) and what the round-trip guarantee is checked
         against (see [[roundtrip]])."""]]

  [child id=json n=3.2 title='JSON projection'
    [intro
      """JSON is the most common interchange surface. CX → JSON mapping
         is straightforward; JSON → CX is well-defined for the JSON
         subset that maps cleanly to CX's data model."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[user :id 42 :email 'ada@example.com' +active
              [roles (admin, editor)]]"""]

    [example lang=json
      """{
           "user": {
             "id": 42,
             "email": "ada@example.com",
             "active": true,
             "roles": ["admin", "editor"]
           }
         }"""]

    [body
      """Attributes become object keys; element bodies of pure-element
         content become nested objects keyed by child element name;
         sequences become arrays; scalars map directly. Mixed content
         (prose with inline elements) is not first-class JSON — it
         projects as an object with a reserved `_cx_prose` key
         containing an ordered list of runs."""]

    [body
      """`cx convert doc.cx --to json` and `cx convert doc.json --to cx`
         are the round-trip pair. See [[roundtrip]] for the guarantees."""]]

  [child id=yaml n=3.3 title='YAML projection'
    [intro
      """YAML follows the JSON projection but uses YAML's mapping and
         sequence syntax. Anchors and merge keys (`<<:`) project to CX's
         `&` / `*` sigils."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[defaults &base :host 'localhost' :tls true]
         [service-a *base :port 8001]
         [service-b *base :port 8002]"""]

    [example lang=yaml
      """defaults: &base
           host: localhost
           tls: true
         service-a:
           <<: *base
           port: 8001
         service-b:
           <<: *base
           port: 8002"""]

    [body
      """YAML's loose schema (untyped scalars, multiple ways to spell the
         same value) is normalized on input: `yes`/`true`/`True` all map
         to canonical `true`, numeric strings stay strings, etc. The
         conversion is documented in
         [`spec/conversions.md`](../../spec/conversions.md)."""]]

  [child id=toml n=3.4 title='TOML projection'
    [intro
      """TOML's table syntax (`[server]`) projects to CX elements; nested
         tables (`[server.tls]`) project to nested elements; arrays of
         tables (`[[users]]`) project to repeated child elements."""]

    [example lang=toml
      """[server]
         host = "localhost"
         tls = true

         [server.tls]
         cert = "site.pem"

         [[users]]
         id = 1
         email = "ada@example.com"

         [[users]]
         id = 2
         email = "grace@example.com" """]

    [example lang=cx
      """[config
           [server :host 'localhost' +tls
             [tls :cert 'site.pem']]
           [users
             [user :id 1 :email 'ada@example.com']
             [user :id 2 :email 'grace@example.com']]]"""]]

  [child id=xml n=3.5 title='XML projection'
    [intro
      """XML is the closest projection — both CX and XML are
         element-with-attributes-and-body languages. The mapping is
         essentially one-to-one for element / attribute / text content."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[user :id 42 :email 'ada@example.com'
           [profile
             [bio """Computing pioneer."""]]]"""]

    [example lang=xml
      """<user id="42" email="ada@example.com">
           <profile>
             <bio>Computing pioneer.</bio>
           </profile>
         </user>"""]

    [body
      """Differences from XML: CX has no DOCTYPE / DTD layer (schemas
         are first-class CX, see [[schema]]); namespace declarations
         are CX attributes with the `xmlns:` prefix preserved; CDATA
         sections project to triple-quoted strings."""]]

  [child id=md n=3.6 title='Markdown projection'
    [intro
      """Markdown projects to CX as structured prose. Headings become
         `[h1] [h2] …`; paragraphs become `[p]`; lists become `[ul]` /
         `[ol]` / `[li]`; inline code becomes `[code]`; links become
         `[link]`. Round-trip is the design center — `cx convert
         README.md --to cx --to md` produces byte-identical output."""]

    [example lang=md
      """# Hello

         A paragraph with *emphasis* and `code`.

         - item 1
         - item 2"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[h1 'Hello']
         [p 'A paragraph with ' [em 'emphasis'] ' and ' [code 'code'] '.']
         [ul
           [li 'item 1']
           [li 'item 2']]"""]]

  [child id=roundtrip n=3.7 title='Round-trip guarantees'
    [intro
      """The round-trip contract is the single most important property of
         CX's surface design. Stated:"""]

    [note
      """For any CX document **D** in canonical form, and any supported
         projection format **F**, the following identity holds:

         `to_cx(to_F(D)) == D` (as canonical bytes)

         For any document **F** in projection format **F** that was
         produced by `to_F(D)` for some canonical CX **D**:

         `to_F(to_cx(F)) == F` (as bytes, modulo format-specific
         whitespace normalization rules in
         [`spec/conversions.md`](../../spec/conversions.md))."""]

    [body
      """The conformance suite includes round-trip fixtures for every
         supported projection. The runner shells out to `cx convert` in
         both directions and asserts byte equality. Drift on any
         projection is a release-blocker."""]

    [body
      """Practically: you can store data as JSON for the JS ecosystem,
         convert to CX for selection / transformation / hashing, then
         convert back to JSON for delivery — no information loss, no
         re-formatting drift, no string-mangling in between."""]]]
